{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat control-reader-brief.md controls/control-packet-10.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage B: reading the control theses (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs, and do not read any file other than this brief\nand the one packet you are given.\n\nEach item of your packet pairs a **ruler question** (a question written by hand for an earlier, frozen measurement, with its\nunit, due day and ladder of rungs) with a **judged question** the system wrote itself for a thesis it opened (its question, the\noutcome it looks at, due day, year and rungs). It also lists **reader B's decisive items**: the evidence an earlier reader, called\nreader B, cited for the frozen answer to the ruler question (each with its day, title, passage and meaning), and **the evidence\nshown to the judge**: every evidence entry the system's judge was shown for the judged question in any of its requests on or\nbefore 2026-07-14 (empty when there is none). You are not told either answer, and you do not judge which answer is right.\n\nFor each item, answer three questions.\n\n1. `same_question`: **yes** when the judged question asks what the ruler question asks: the same quantity measured the same way\n   (the same total, scope and unit: not a part of it, a ceiling on it, another quantity or another unit), or the same event,\n   for the same period. **no** otherwise. Wording may differ; a judged question that asks for more or less than the ruler's,\n   or for another period, is **no**.\n2. `same_due`: for an **event** (a yes-or-no question), **yes** only when the judged question's deadline is the ruler's\n   deadline. For a **figure**, **yes** when the two due days both mark the publication of the same period's figure (for\n   example, both the annual report of the same fiscal year, even if the two days differ); **no** otherwise.\n3. `items`: for **each** of reader B's decisive items, by its id, **shown** when its content is present in the evidence shown to\n   the judge (the same fact or figure, in any wording, from any source), **not_shown** when it is absent. Search the entries for\n   the figure or the fact; do not mark an item shown because a related item is.\n\nReturn one JSON object per item, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else (no prose, no code fence):\n`{\"control\": 3, \"same_question\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"same_due\": \"yes\"|\"no\", \"items\": {\"i005\": \"shown\"|\"not_shown\", ...}, \"why\": \"one sentence naming what decides each no and each not_shown\"}`.\n\n## Third reader (only where the two readers differ)\n\nA third reader of each family is given this brief, the packet, and a list of the disputed points, each a control number and a\nfield (`same_question`, `same_due`, or `item <id>`), without the two readers' answers. For each point, answer that one question\nafresh by the rules above and return one JSON object per point, one per line, and nothing else:\n`{\"control\": 3, \"field\": \"item i005\", \"ruling\": \"yes\"|\"no\"|\"shown\"|\"not_shown\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}` (`yes`/`no` for the two\nquestion fields, `shown`/`not_shown` for an item).\n[\n {\n  \"control\": 23,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\",\n   \"unit\": \"percent (75 means 75%)\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-02-28\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 77%.\",\n     \"lo\": 77.0,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 75% but below 77%.\",\n     \"lo\": 75.0,\n     \"hi\": 77.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 73% but below 75%, compared with the article's 75% reference level.\",\n     \"lo\": 73.0,\n     \"hi\": 75.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is at least 71% but below 73%.\",\n     \"lo\": 71.0,\n     \"hi\": 73.0\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"FY2027 non-GAAP gross margin is below 71%.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": 71.0\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v15\",\n   \"question\": \"What will NVIDIA's gross margin be in FY2027?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"NVIDIA gross margin\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-02-28\",\n   \"year\": \"FY2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2027 gross margin is at least 78%.\",\n    \"lo\": 78.0,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2027 gross margin is at least 76% and below 78%.\",\n    \"lo\": 76.0,\n    \"hi\": 78.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2027 gross margin is at least 74% and below 76%.\",\n    \"lo\": 74.0,\n    \"hi\": 76.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2027 gross margin is at least 72% and below 74%.\",\n    \"lo\": 72.0,\n    \"hi\": 74.0\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"condition\": \"FY2027 gross margin is below 72%.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": 72.0\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i281\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-09\",\n    \"title\": \"DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow\",\n    \"passage\": \"forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Q2 FY2027 revenue and gross-margin guidance\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$91 billion ±2%; non-GAAP gross margin about 75%\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 FY2027\",\n     \"condition\": \"assumes no China Data Center compute revenue\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i153\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\",\n    \"passage\": \"Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"what\": \"Q2 FY2027 revenue and gross margin guidance\",\n     \"how_much\": \"$91.0B; 75%\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 FY2027\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\",\n     \"status_note\": \"guidance\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i163\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?\",\n    \"passage\": \"Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA reported\",\n     \"what\": \"GAAP gross margin and operating margin vs Micron\",\n     \"how_much\": \"74.9%; ~66%\",\n     \"period\": \"latest quarter (Q1 FY2027)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i217\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Morgan Stanley Research\",\n     \"what\": \"memory share of rack bill of materials; memory content per rack, driven by 3x LPDDR5X (54 TB per rack) and about $1 million or more of 3D NAND\",\n     \"how_much\": \"memory 5–10% (GB200) to 25–30% (VR200); about $2 million per rack, up 435%; GPU share 63% to 51%\",\n     \"status\": \"forecast\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e374 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] NVIDIA maintained its gross margin outlook in the mid-70% range.\\n    Quote: maintained its gross margin outlook in the mid-70% range\",\n   \"- e374 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dedacecd6d2ed5192c307199 2026-07-13] NVIDIA maintained its gross margin outlook in the mid-70% range.\\n    Quote: maintained its gross margin outlook in the mid-70% range\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e440; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e426 [third_party_view; Vandita Jadeja; original source Vandita Jadeja 2026-07-14; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] The author cites NVIDIA forward EPS of $8, a 25x forward P/E, revenue growth above 85% year over year, and gross margins of 75%.\\n    Quote: At $203.53 against forward EPS of $8, Nvidia’s forward P/E 25x is not expensive for a company compounding revenue north of 85% YoY with 75% gross margins.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e262; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e440 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-14; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The author said NVIDIA's revenue was growing 85% and its gross margins were 75%.\\n    Quote: Paying 22x forward earnings for a business growing revenue 85% with 75% gross margins is rare\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e374; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e464 [third_party_view; ebc.com; original source ebc.com ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] The article says NVIDIA has committed $119 billion to secure its supply chain and reports that rising HBM costs raise concerns about its long-term gross margins.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has committed a staggering $119 billion to secure its supply chain, but the soaring costs of HBM are raising alarms about the company's long-term gross margins.\",\n   \"- e465 [third_party_view; ebc.com; original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Bank of America analysts argued that NVIDIA’s pricing power can offset rising component expenses, including HBM costs.\\n    Quote: While analysts from Bank of America argue that Nvidia’s pricing power can easily offset these component expenses, the mere whisper of compressed margins has been enough to trigger automated sell programs.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
